#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "11f5bedf-cfc8-5ace-b080-5ec30b406a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7436" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/875/Source/Images/e77c2a7f7fa0a1f9a45e1aa139b3a543.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0)$, and passes through the point $(4,8)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8)$, we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{4 = 2pt; 8 = pt^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= 2pt \\\\\n2 &= pt \\\\\nt &= \\frac{2}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 &= p(\\frac{2}{p})^{2} \\\\\n8 &= p \\cdot \\frac{4}{p^{2}} \\\\\n8 &= \\frac{4}{p} \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "29cfe1ff-ca64-51f9-8da1-5d0f798711f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7437" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4px &= -64x \\\\\n4p &= -64 \\\\\np &= -16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "07678ad9-a16f-5fd2-8e39-85376aa9112e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7442" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= \\frac{x}{2}, t = \\frac{y}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= \\frac{y^{2}}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nand rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\ny^{2} &= 8x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66c4d7b-b7e0-5427-aa09-67081702b893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087169} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf1d1aa-8710-52d1-b04f-7f24051c8c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{1}{8}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087170} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b1dd9f-3c72-53f9-b46f-31d8b8e53972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087171} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4b5790-ec0d-598d-8c52-1bf68082f1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{1}{8}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087172} {:answer/id #uuid "5c99c07f-958d-5da7-897e-51e78448baa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66c4d7b-b7e0-5427-aa09-67081702b893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087169} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b8130c0-7afd-5ddc-8dcc-1799688a8dc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087168}] :question/id #uuid "4ceae8e0-efff-58d4-996b-0a6fa5e6da13" :question/math-academy-id "q-84107" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x =-\\frac{1}{4}t, y =-\\frac{1}{8}t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9171e1f2-a4c2-5f46-b8dc-a3634778f143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{3}{2}t, y = \\